WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.340
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:04.640
A straw can look bent in water without actually bending.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.160
The light changes direction at the boundary, and your eyes trace the arriving

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.000
rays backward.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.740
That is our starting point for refraction.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.989
Refractive index, n, is the vacuum speed of light divided by its phase speed

00:00:17.989 --> 00:00:18.100
in

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:18.560
the medium.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.760
A larger index means a smaller speed, not necessarily a larger mass density.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.440
Draw the normal at the crossing point.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:32.120
Snell's law says n one times sine of the incidence angle equals n two times

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.440
sine of the refraction angle.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.900
Both angles are measured from the normal.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.160
If light enters glass from air at thirty degrees, with glass index one point

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.860
five, sine of r is one third.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.080
So r is about nineteen point five degrees.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.100
It bends toward the normal.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.360
Why doesn't the frequency change?

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.300
At a stationary boundary, successive oscillations must match on both sides.

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:00.480
The frequency stays fixed, while speed and wavelength change.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:05.540
With glass index one point five, a vacuum wavelength of six hundred nanometers

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.960
becomes four hundred nanometers in glass.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.780
At normal incidence the ray does not turn, even though its speed changes.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:15.880
So refraction does not always mean a visible bend.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:21.283
Our calculations assume ordinary transparent isotropic media and a stated

00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:21.620
index

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:22.580
at the light's frequency.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.560
Let's send a ray through a glass slab with parallel faces.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.160
At entry it bends toward the normal.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.380
At exit it bends away.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.540
Because the faces are parallel and the outside medium is the same on both

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:39.140
sides, the outgoing angle equals the original incoming angle.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:43.628
The emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray, but it is displaced

00:01:43.628 --> 00:01:44.120
sideways.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.180
That last distinction is a favorite trap: parallel does not mean the very same

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:49.440
line.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.160
On our board, extend the incident line straight ahead.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.080
The shortest perpendicular distance between that line and the emerging ray is

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:58.980
the lateral shift.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:04.788
For slab thickness t, measured perpendicular to its faces, the shift is t

00:02:04.788 --> 00:02:05.120
times

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.480
sine of i minus r, divided by cosine r.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.020
Suppose the slab is two centimeters thick, the incidence angle is forty five

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.800
degrees, and refraction inside is thirty degrees.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.640
Its index relative to the outside is about one point four one four.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.580
The shift is two times sine fifteen divided by cosine thirty, about zero point

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:26.620
six zero centimeters.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.420
Larger thickness increases this shift at fixed angles.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:35.560
At normal incidence, i and r are both zero, and the lateral shift vanishes.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:40.340
Remember the assumptions: parallel faces and the same outside index.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:44.640
A prism has nonparallel faces, so its ray need not emerge parallel.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.840
We'll give prisms their own class.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.200
Now imagine looking almost vertically down at a small object in water.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.400
Light leaving the water bends away from the normal on entering air.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:00.680
Your eye assumes a straight path and traces those rays backward to a point

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.780
closer to the surface.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.960
The apparent position is shallower than the actual object.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:11.440
For near normal viewing at a plane interface, apparent depth equals real depth

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.960
times the observer medium's index divided by the object medium's index.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.580
Water has index four thirds and air is approximately one.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.480
A twelve centimeter depth therefore appears as nine centimeters.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.700
The apparent upward shift is three centimeters.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.320
Let's see why that ratio makes sense.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.780
Snell's law connects the angles.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:36.800
For small angles, sine and tangent are approximately the same.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.500
The same sideways ray displacement, divided by two different depths, gives

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:42.520
those tangent angles.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.760
Combining the geometry and Snell's law gives the index ratio.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:51.220
That is an approximation with a reason, not a rule for arbitrary viewing

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:51.720
directions.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:56.900
If the observer is instead inside a higher index medium, the ratio can exceed

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:57.260
one.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:01.380
Do not automatically divide by whatever index appears in the question.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:06.240
Identify where the object is, where the observer is, and whether the near

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:07.960
normal approximation is justified.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:13.420
In multilayer questions, each layer's apparent contribution can be added only

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.000
within the matching paraxial setup.

00:04:15.192 --> 00:04:20.012
What happens as a ray inside glass meets air at larger and larger incidence

00:04:20.012 --> 00:04:20.352
angles?

00:04:21.212 --> 00:04:24.372
Snell's law predicts a refracted angle larger than the incident one.

00:04:25.372 --> 00:04:27.812
Eventually the refracted angle reaches ninety degrees.

00:04:28.392 --> 00:04:31.072
The corresponding incidence angle is the critical angle.

00:04:31.912 --> 00:04:34.872
Beyond it, the equation would demand a sine larger than one.

00:04:35.392 --> 00:04:39.492
That means no ordinary propagating refracted ray exists in the lower index

00:04:39.492 --> 00:04:39.872
medium.

00:04:40.252 --> 00:04:42.152
We have total internal reflection.

00:04:43.012 --> 00:04:47.032
Check both conditions: the ray must approach from higher index toward lower

00:04:47.032 --> 00:04:49.912
index, and the angle must exceed the critical angle.

00:04:50.772 --> 00:04:54.992
For glass index one point five facing air, sine of the critical angle is two

00:04:54.992 --> 00:04:55.172
thirds.

00:04:55.932 --> 00:04:58.012
The angle is about forty one point eight degrees.

00:04:58.692 --> 00:05:03.018
At fifty degrees we get total internal reflection; at thirty degrees we do

00:05:03.018 --> 00:05:03.252
not.

00:05:04.052 --> 00:05:07.732
Exactly at the critical angle is the grazing limit, not the same as strictly

00:05:07.732 --> 00:05:08.232
above it.

00:05:08.752 --> 00:05:13.012
A core of higher index surrounded by lower index cladding can guide light

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:14.832
through repeated total internal reflection.

00:05:15.712 --> 00:05:18.052
That is the simple ray picture of an optical fiber.

00:05:18.832 --> 00:05:22.372
The incidence angle at the core boundary is measured from that boundary's

00:05:22.372 --> 00:05:24.992
normal, not automatically from the fiber axis.

00:05:25.672 --> 00:05:28.712
Our ideal explanation assumes a lossless interface.

00:05:29.572 --> 00:05:33.932
Real fibers have losses, and wave optics includes an evanescent field outside.

00:05:34.892 --> 00:05:37.812
Neither detail changes our basic JEE threshold check.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.640
A curved refracting boundary can form an image, even before we have a complete

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:43.000
lens.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:48.058
Use the Cartesian convention: incident light travels left to right, the

00:05:48.058 --> 00:05:48.480
surface

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.740
pole is the origin, and rightward distances are positive.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:57.729
Object distance is u, image distance is v, and radius R points from the pole

00:05:57.729 --> 00:05:57.860
to

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:58.860
the center of curvature.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:05.220
The equation is n two over v minus n one over u equals n two minus

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:06.960
n one, divided by R.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:11.420
This relation applies to paraxial rays at a single spherical interface.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:16.440
Let's try air entering a semi infinite glass medium with index one point five.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:21.800
The real object is sixty centimeters to the left, so u is negative sixty.

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:25.860
The center of curvature is twenty centimeters to the right, so R is positive

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:26.820
twenty.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:31.120
Our right side is zero point five over twenty, or one fortieth per centimeter.

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:36.640
On the left, subtracting one over negative sixty adds one sixtieth.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:41.473
Thus one point five over v is one one hundred twentieth, and v is positive one

00:06:41.473 --> 00:06:42.440
hundred eighty centimeters.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:45.740
The refracted rays converge inside the glass.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:51.520
If we set R to infinity, the curvature term becomes zero, recovering the plane

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:52.240
interface limit.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:55.180
Be careful: this is not the mirror equation.

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.240
And if another boundary follows, its refraction must be handled too.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:02.700
That's exactly the bridge to our next class on lenses.
